================================================================================
SOURCES OF IDEAS AND FORMAT KNOWLEDGE  (no code from them is included)
================================================================================

The Keychain tab and the opening of locked notes were written independently
from what the following publish about the formats. They are credited here, in
AUTHORS and in README:

iphone-dataprotection (Jean-Baptiste Bedrune and Jean Sigwald, Sogeti ESEC Lab;
    BSD 3-Clause): the layout of keychain items in an encrypted backup.
    https://github.com/dinosec/iphone-dataprotection
Apple Cloud Notes Parser (Jon Baumann, Ciofeca Forensics, threeplanetssoftware; MIT License): where
    locked notes and their attachments keep their lock details, and how they
    are opened. https://github.com/threeplanetssoftware/apple_cloud_notes_parser
hashcat (the hashcat developers; MIT License): the Apple Secure Notes module
    (hash mode 16200). https://hashcat.net/hashcat/
Apple Platform Security Guide (Apple Inc.): the documented design of locked
    notes and of keychain data protection.
RFC 3394 and NIST SP 800-38D: the AES key wrap and AES-GCM standards.
kinnay/AEA, python-aea (Yannik Marchand; MIT License): an implementation of the
    Apple Encrypted Archive format that the reader for the files in Health's
    clinical records was checked against. https://github.com/kinnay/AEA
ipsw (blacktop; MIT License): its AEA decoder describes the key derivation
    steps of the format. https://github.com/blacktop/ipsw
The Apple Wiki: its page on the Apple Encrypted Archive format.
HL7 FHIR (https://hl7.org/fhir) and Apple's HealthKit documentation: the names
    and values used to read Health records, symptoms and cycle tracking.

